function GradientBrainIcon() {
  return (
    <div style={{ position: 'relative', display: 'inline-block' }}>
      <Brain 
        size={32}
        style={{
          background: 'linear-gradient(45deg, #9c36b5, #6c5ce7, #a29bfe)',
          WebkitBackgroundClip: 'text',
          backgroundClip: 'text',
          color: 'transparent',
          stroke: 'url(#gradient)' // This might not work for all icons
        }}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Option 5: Create SVG Component with Gradient (if you can extract the SVG)

If you can copy the SVG code from your browser's developer tools:

1. Right-click on the SVG in your browser
2. Inspect element
3. Copy the `<svg>` element
4. Create a React component:

```tsx
function GradientBrainIcon() {
  return (
    <svg width="32" height="32" viewBox="0 0 100 100" fill="none">
      <defs>
        <linearGradient id="brain-gradient" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="100%" y2="100%">
          <stop offset="0%" stopColor="#9c36b5" />
          <stop offset="50%" stopColor="#6c5ce7" />
          <stop offset="100%" stopColor="#a29bfe" />
        </linearGradient>
      </defs>
      {/* Paste your SVG paths here and replace stroke="black" with stroke="url(#brain-gradient)" */}
    </svg>
  );
}
```

**I recommend Option 1 (CSS Mask)** as it's the simplest and most reliable. Just replace `/path-to-your-brain-icon.svg` with the actual path to your SVG file!

