 Looking at your Footer.tsx file, I see that you're trying to use `c="customBlue.6"` to set the text color to your custom color, but it's not working. Let me explain why and how to fix it.

The issue is that "customBlue" isn't defined in your theme yet, or it's not properly set up. Let me walk you through how to get your custom colors working:

## The Problem

In your Footer component:
```tsx
<Text fz="xl" fw={700} c="customBlue.6">
  Skillfully Human
</Text>
```

You're trying to use a color called "customBlue" at shade 6, but Mantine doesn't recognize this color because:

1. Either your theme doesn't have a color defined with the name "customBlue"
2. Or the theme isn't properly applied to your application

## The Solution

### 1. First, make sure your theme is properly defined:

```typescript
// theme/index.ts
import { createTheme, MantineColorsTuple } from '@mantine/core';

// Define your custom blue color with all 10 shades
const customBlue: MantineColorsTuple = [
  '#E7F5FF', // 0: lightest
  '#D0EBFF', // 1
  '#A5D8FF', // 2
  '#74C0FC', // 3
  '#4DABF7', // 4
  '#339AF0', // 5
  '#228BE6', // 6
  '#1C7ED6', // 7
  '#1971C2', // 8
  '#1864AB'  // 9: darkest
];

// Create and export your theme
export const theme = createTheme({
  // Add your custom colors to the theme
  colors: {
    customBlue: customBlue,
    // Add any other custom colors here
  },
  // You can also set the primary color if desired
  primaryColor: 'customBlue',
});
```

### 2. Make sure your theme is properly applied to your application:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx (for Next.js App Router)
import { MantineProvider } from '@mantine/core';
import { theme } from '../theme';

export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <MantineProvider theme={theme}>
          {children}
        </MantineProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

### 3. TypeScript Type Declaration (if you're using TypeScript):

To avoid TypeScript errors, you should also declare your custom colors in a type declaration file:

```typescript
// types/mantine.d.ts
import { MantineColorsTuple } from '@mantine/core';

declare module '@mantine/core' {
  export interface MantineThemeColorsOverride {
    customBlue: MantineColorsTuple;
    // Add any other custom colors here
  }
}
```

### 4. Alternative Approach - Use the Color in Your Component:

If you're having issues with the custom color name, you can also directly use your custom color in the component:

```tsx
// In your Footer.tsx
const customBlueColor = '#228BE6'; // This is shade 6 of your customBlue

return (
  // ...
  <Text fz="xl" fw={700} c={customBlueColor}>
    Skillfully Human
  </Text>
  // ...
);
```

Or use the theme object directly:

```tsx
// In your Footer.tsx
const theme = useMantineTheme();

// Then use it in your JSX
<Text fz="xl" fw={700} c={theme.colors.customBlue[6]}>
  Skillfully Human
</Text>
```

### 5. Check for Typos and Case Sensitivity:

Make sure the color name in your theme definition exactly matches what you're using in your component. "customBlue" and "CustomBlue" would be treated as different colors.

### 6. Debug Your Theme:

You can add a simple debugging component to verify your theme is working:

```tsx
const theme = useMantineTheme();
console.log('Available colors:', Object.keys(theme.colors));
console.log('customBlue exists:', 'customBlue' in theme.colors);
console.log('customBlue value:', theme.colors.customBlue);
```

This will help you confirm whether your custom colors are properly defined in the theme.

By following these steps, your `c="customBlue.6"` should work correctly in your Footer component. Let me know if you need any further clarification or assistance!

