 This is a common issue when setting up custom colors in Mantine. Let me explain what's happening and how to properly add custom colors without losing the built-in ones.

## The Problem

When you define custom colors in your Mantine theme, you're likely overriding the entire `colors` object rather than extending it. The colors property in the theme completely replaces the default colors rather than merging with them.

## The Solution

You need to include both your custom colors AND the default colors. Here's how to properly set up your theme:

```typescript
// theme/index.ts
import { createTheme, DEFAULT_THEME } from '@mantine/core';

// Create your custom colors
const customBlue = [
  '#E7F5FF', // 0
  '#D0EBFF', // 1
  '#A5D8FF', // 2
  '#74C0FC', // 3
  '#4DABF7', // 4
  '#339AF0', // 5
  '#228BE6', // 6
  '#1C7ED6', // 7
  '#1971C2', // 8
  '#1864AB'  // 9
];

// Create your theme by extending the default theme
export const theme = createTheme({
  colors: {
    // Include all the default colors
    ...DEFAULT_THEME.colors,

    // Add your custom colors
    customBlue: customBlue,
    // Any other custom colors...
  },
  // Other theme customizations...
});
```

This approach ensures that you:
1. Keep all the built-in Mantine colors (blue, red, green, etc.)
2. Add your custom colors alongside them
3. Can override specific built-in colors if needed (by redefining them after spreading the defaults)

## Alternative Approach

If you're using TypeScript and want a slightly more explicit approach:

```typescript
import { createTheme, DEFAULT_THEME } from '@mantine/core';

export const theme = createTheme({
  colors: {
    // Include all the default colors explicitly
    blue: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.blue,
    cyan: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.cyan,
    gray: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.gray,
    green: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.green,
    indigo: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.indigo,
    orange: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.orange,
    pink: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.pink,
    red: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.red,
    teal: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.teal,
    violet: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.violet,
    yellow: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.yellow,
    dark: DEFAULT_THEME.colors.dark,

    // Add or override with your custom colors
    customBlue: [
      '#E7F5FF', '#D0EBFF', '#A5D8FF', '#74C0FC', '#4DABF7',
      '#339AF0', '#228BE6', '#1C7ED6', '#1971C2', '#1864AB'
    ],
  },
});
```

## Debug Your Theme

You can add this code somewhere in your application to verify your theme has all the expected colors:

```tsx
import { useMantineTheme } from '@mantine/core';

function ThemeDebugger() {
  const theme = useMantineTheme();
  console.log('Available colors:', Object.keys(theme.colors));
  return null;
}

// Include this component in your app temporarily
```

## When Overriding Built-in Colors

If you want to override a built-in color with your own version, you can do so like this:

```typescript
export const theme = createTheme({
  colors: {
    // Include all default colors
    ...DEFAULT_THEME.colors,

    // Override the built-in blue with your custom blue
    blue: [
      '#E7F5FF', '#D0EBFF', '#A5D8FF', '#74C0FC', '#4DABF7',
      '#339AF0', '#228BE6', '#1C7ED6', '#1971C2', '#1864AB'
    ],

    // Add completely new custom colors
    brandPrimary: [/* your 10 shades here */],
  },
});
```

By following these approaches, you should be able to have both your custom colors AND the built-in Mantine colors available in your application.

