 # Fixing the Hydration Error in Next.js with Mantine

This error is a classic React hydration mismatch. Let me explain what's happening and how to fix it:

## The Problem

The error shows that the server-rendered HTML and client-rendered HTML don't match. Specifically, the server is rendering:

```html
<html lang="en">
```

But the client is trying to render:

```html
<html lang="en" data-mantine-color-scheme="light">
```

The attribute `data-mantine-color-scheme="light"` is being added by Mantine on the client side but wasn't present in the server-rendered HTML.

## Cause of the Issue

This happens because Mantine's `MantineProvider` is adding the color scheme attribute when it runs on the client side, but this attribute wasn't present in the server-rendered HTML.

## Solution

The fix is to ensure that Mantine properly handles Server Side Rendering (SSR) by following these steps:

### 1. Update your Root Layout Component

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
import { MantineProvider, ColorSchemeScript } from '@mantine/core';
import { theme } from '../theme';

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <head>
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
        {/* Add this to ensure server-client matching */}
        <ColorSchemeScript defaultColorScheme="light" />
      </head>
      <body>
        <MantineProvider theme={theme} defaultColorScheme="light">
          {children}
        </MantineProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

The key changes are:

1. Adding `<ColorSchemeScript defaultColorScheme="light" />` in the `<head>` section
2. Setting the same `defaultColorScheme="light"` on the `MantineProvider`

### 2. Ensure Client-Side Only Initialization for Color Scheme Changes

If you have any color scheme switching functionality, make sure it's properly client-side only:

```tsx
'use client';

import { useColorScheme } from '@mantine/hooks';
// Rest of your color scheme switching component
```

### 3. Add Proper Next.js Metadata

For Next.js App Router, consider using the metadata API for better integration:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
import { Metadata } from 'next';

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'Your App',
  description: 'Your app description',
  // Other metadata
};
```

## Why This Works

The `ColorSchemeScript` component adds a script that sets the `data-mantine-color-scheme` attribute during server-side rendering, ensuring that the HTML generated on the server will match what the client expects to see when hydration occurs.

## Additional Troubleshooting Steps

If you're still experiencing hydration errors after making these changes:

### 1. Check for Dynamic Content

Look for components that use:
- `Date.now()`
- `Math.random()`
- `new Date()`
- Browser-only APIs like `window` or `localStorage`

Wrap such components with:
```tsx
'use client';

import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

function DynamicComponent() {
  const [mounted, setMounted] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setMounted(true);
  }, []);

  if (!mounted) return null; // or a placeholder

  // Your dynamic content here
}
```

### 2. Check for Inconsistent Mantine Configuration

Make sure your theme setup is consistent across your application:
- Same color scheme settings
- Same theme object
- No conflicting MantineProvider instances

### 3. Check Next.js Configuration

Ensure your `next.config.js` doesn't have any settings that might interfere with SSR:

```js
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  reactStrictMode: true,
  // Other settings...
};

module.exports = nextConfig;
```

## Key Takeaway

The most important fix is adding the `ColorSchemeScript` to the `<head>` section of your document with the same `defaultColorScheme` that you're using in your `MantineProvider`. This ensures the server and client render the same initial HTML structure.

Let me know if this resolves your hydration error!

