 Ah, I see the issue! The site's main layout is still being applied to your dashboard page. You have a few options to fix this:

## Option 1: Create a separate route group for admin (Recommended)

Create this folder structure:
```
app/
├── (site)/           # Route group for main site
│   ├── layout.tsx    # Main site layout
│   ├── page.tsx      # Homepage
│   └── about/        # Other site pages...
└── (admin)/          # Route group for admin
    ├── layout.tsx    # Admin layout (minimal or none)
    └── dashboard/
        └── page.tsx  # Your dashboard page
```

Your `app/(admin)/layout.tsx` can be minimal:
```tsx
export default function AdminLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return <>{children}</>;  // No wrapping layout
}
```

## Option 2: Use route groups to exclude from main layout

Move your current pages into a route group and keep admin separate:
```
app/
├── layout.tsx        # Root layout (minimal)
├── (main)/          # Main site pages
│   ├── layout.tsx   # Your current site layout
│   ├── page.tsx     # Homepage
│   └── ...
└── admin/
    └── dashboard/
        └── page.tsx  # Dashboard without main layout
```

## Option 3: Conditionally render layout based on pathname

In your main `layout.tsx`:
```tsx
'use client';
import { usePathname } from 'next/navigation';

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  const pathname = usePathname();
  const isAdmin = pathname?.startsWith('/admin');

  if (isAdmin) {
    return (
      <html lang="en">
        <body>{children}</body>
      </html>
    );
  }

  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <Header />
        <main>{children}</main>
        <Footer />
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

Which approach would you prefer? Option 1 with route groups is usually the cleanest solution.

